1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:01,460
Let's do the demo.

2
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:05,460
First, we install the software on a Windows PC.
I'll skip the installation itself.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:13,660
Once it's installed, you get an address
you can open, and then you can start using it.

4
00:00:13,700 --> 00:00:20,526
This is a VM and its display isn't great,
so I'll use my Mac for the demo.

5
00:00:20,566 --> 00:00:28,126
My Mac reaches this VM through another
internal address it's mapped to,

6
00:00:28,166 --> 00:00:33,800
so anything I do in the browser on my Mac
is the same as doing it on this machine.

7
00:00:33,866 --> 00:00:40,760
Let's go to the browser on the Mac
and open it.

8
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:45,166
Once it's open, you can see
the first step is to connect to FMC.

9
00:00:45,400 --> 00:00:52,660
Here's how the tool works:
first, install it on your Windows PC,

10
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:59,666
then open it and use it to connect to FMC,
the Firewall Management Center.

11
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:07,093
Then open the app you want to detect.

12
00:01:07,133 --> 00:01:13,893
The tool first captures the connections
created by that app's processes,

13
00:01:13,933 --> 00:01:19,500
then searches FMC for the matching events,

14
00:01:19,566 --> 00:01:25,866
so you can see what the firewall detected.

15
00:01:26,333 --> 00:01:29,500
It correlates the two, and then you can
generate the matching Lua script.

16
00:01:31,166 --> 00:01:35,700
Let's take a look. First, connect.
Click Test: OK, connected.

17
00:01:35,800 --> 00:01:40,833
Next, I'll reopen this application.

18
00:01:41,066 --> 00:01:48,000
I've reopened the app.
Now back to this screen.

19
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:52,426
I'll select the process to monitor,
which is this app's process.

20
00:01:52,466 --> 00:01:59,066
You'll see it once the app is open in Windows,
so I won't go into that.

21
00:02:00,666 --> 00:02:06,093
It starts collecting, and you can see
the connections from that process.

22
00:02:06,133 --> 00:02:11,693
But since the app was already open,
many connections were made at startup.

23
00:02:11,733 --> 00:02:18,900
To capture everything, I need to
close the app and run it again.

24
00:02:21,466 --> 00:02:26,866
I'll restart the China Merchants Securities
app I'm testing and log in again.

25
00:02:28,966 --> 00:02:34,660
I'll log in the simplest way. After login,
it should generate new traffic.

26
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:38,733
Back in the detector, you can see
it has generated new traffic.

27
00:02:38,866 --> 00:02:44,626
While collecting, it's also correlating
with the FMC events,

28
00:02:44,666 --> 00:02:47,960
but there's a delay between a connection
being created and being correlated.

29
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:51,333
See, it's correlated now.
Let's wait a bit.

30
00:02:53,266 --> 00:02:55,893
It's making lots of connections,
especially at startup.

31
00:02:55,933 --> 00:03:01,166
Once the app settles, it only has two
connections, but startup creates many.

32
00:03:04,800 --> 00:03:06,700
Once it stops making new ones,

33
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:11,500
after capturing for a while,
I can click Stop.

34
00:03:12,100 --> 00:03:14,400
I built in a countdown here,

35
00:03:14,866 --> 00:03:22,466
because after the app makes a connection,
querying its firewall log in FMC takes time.

36
00:03:22,666 --> 00:03:27,360
It doesn't show up right away;
it usually takes over ten seconds.

37
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:31,533
So to make sure the data lines up,
I usually count down 30 seconds.

38
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:37,266
See, almost everything is correlated.

39
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:42,293
When the countdown finishes,
you can generate the Lua.

40
00:03:42,333 --> 00:03:44,926
Until then, this button is grayed out.

41
00:03:44,966 --> 00:03:48,466
Now click Generate Lua,
and you can see

42
00:03:49,533 --> 00:03:54,493
all the signatures the firewall detected
are listed here.

43
00:03:54,533 --> 00:03:57,166
It uses them to build the rules.

44
00:03:57,266 --> 00:04:02,726
But there's a catch: the domains it sees,
such as the SNI or host,

45
00:04:02,766 --> 00:04:05,700
may sit behind a CDN,
especially the host.

46
00:04:05,866 --> 00:04:10,400
If we add a CDN to the signature,
it can cause false positives,

47
00:04:10,466 --> 00:04:13,760
because another app may sit
behind the same CDN.

48
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:19,293
If they share a domain, when you block
or identify one app,

49
00:04:19,333 --> 00:04:23,460
the other app gets tagged too,
which isn't accurate.

50
00:04:23,500 --> 00:04:29,200
So you need to exclude CDNs.
Many CDNs are easy to spot,

51
00:04:29,500 --> 00:04:34,133
click to exclude them. It's called
Exclude as CDN; just check the box.

52
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:37,200
There aren't any here.

53
00:04:37,866 --> 00:04:43,126
We keep a history of excluded CDNs,
so CDN knowledge builds up over time,

54
00:04:43,166 --> 00:04:47,326
and next time you generate, it will
suggest which ones are likely CDNs.

55
00:04:47,366 --> 00:04:49,566
OK.

56
00:04:52,300 --> 00:04:57,460
Let's rename it
so it's easier to remember.

57
00:04:57,500 --> 00:04:58,800
Now click Generate.

58
00:05:02,333 --> 00:05:06,126
Done. After generating,
it also tells you

59
00:05:06,166 --> 00:05:13,260
how to import this Lua script into FMC.

60
00:05:13,300 --> 00:05:18,660
Let's look again. This is the traffic
captured locally on Windows,

61
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:24,133
and this is what FMC saw.
You can see how they correlate.

62
00:05:24,666 --> 00:05:28,200
The Lua is generated from what
the firewall detected.

63
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:32,366
You can select any previously
generated Lua here,

64
00:05:32,800 --> 00:05:36,893
and it shows the auto-generated script,
so you don't need to touch it.

65
00:05:36,933 --> 00:05:41,226
If you know Lua, you can read it,
but there's no need.

66
00:05:41,266 --> 00:05:48,226
Once the Lua is generated, import it
into the firewall and deploy it.

67
00:05:48,266 --> 00:05:52,826
After that, close the app, reopen it,
and check whether it matches.

68
00:05:52,866 --> 00:05:56,533
Let's look at one I did earlier.

69
00:06:05,466 --> 00:06:10,460
You can see our custom app is working.

70
00:06:10,500 --> 00:06:15,900
You can also look it up here:
there's an FMC Connection page.

71
00:06:16,166 --> 00:06:24,600
Click it to query, and you'll see
the matching application info.

72
00:06:24,966 --> 00:06:28,260
The UI supports both Chinese and English.

73
00:06:28,300 --> 00:06:34,300
The install package includes a README
in both Chinese and English.

74
00:06:34,533 --> 00:06:42,600
It explains how to install, what changes
it makes to Windows, and what it loads.

75
00:06:42,800 --> 00:06:47,133
Both languages are included. Then click
Start to install, and you're done.
